.he "cp (1)                       8/12/26                       cp (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
cp
- copy files
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
cp [ -is ] f1 f2
cp [ -irs ] f1 ... fn directory
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
In the first form cp copies f1 onto f2.  In the second it copies each
file into the named directory, keeping its name.
.sp
Cp refuses to copy a file onto itself.  It compares the device and the
i-number to decide, so it catches the case reached by two different
paths as well as the obvious one.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-i	Ask before overwriting a file that exists.

.ti -10
-r	Copy a directory, and everything under it, recursively.

.ti -10
-s	Leave holes for whole blocks of zeros.  A block of the source that
is all zero is seeked over in the copy instead of written, so the copy's
block list carries no block for it.  This is what lets a boot loader skip
the zero pages of a kernel image.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ls (1), rm (1), mkdir (1)
